  "id": "runner-selector-space",
  "detail": "`role:<role>#<idx>` is the product's element identity: a snapshot ASSIGNS the index and a later tap, type or clip-box resolution READS it back. Those halves ran different code. Twelve copies of the in-page `interactive()` predicate carried four incompatible tappable grammars, and Electron and Tauri each shipped more than one at once, so the same selector named different elements inside one run. On a page with an unkeyed `<input type=text>` then an unkeyed `<textarea>`, the Tauri snapshot walk (tauri-snapshot/part-01.mjs) excluded input types text/password/email/number/search, so it called the textarea `role:textfield#0` and left the input unaddressable, while the Tauri ground-truth walk (tauri/part-02.mjs) included both and called the textarea `role:textfield#1`. Electron's snapshot excluded them while its own gtCollect included them, and its typeInto carried a THIRD vocabulary with no tappable filter at all. A finding recorded in one index space and replayed through another does not point at the element it names, which is the reproduction claim itself. The excluding rule was not even the rule it claimed to be: `<textarea>` and `<select>` still entered through its `tabIndex >= 0` fallback. The canonical grammar is the including one: web, the only internally consistent tier, used it at all four of its sites, and the explorer drives a field with a `type` action, so excluding fields collapses a form-gated app to one dead state. Keeping both as a capability difference was rejected, since the Electron renderer is Chromium and the Tauri webview is a full DOM, so both rules run identically on all three. The content-bug oracle had the same defect: web keyed an unkeyed element by document-order tag index while the Electron and Tauri copies did `if (!key) continue`, so a bare `<span>[object Object]</span>` produced NO finding on two of three DOM runners while content-bug was declared evaluated on all nine in validation/oracles/coverage.json, and an absent finding reads exactly like a clean result. tap() diverged the same way: web clicks the element centre with a real driver pointer, Electron called el.click(), which dispatches an untrusted event at the node, skipping hit-testing, so it succeeds on a control an overlay completely covers; it also called el.focus() first, which focusLossCheck ignores, so it bought nothing while parking focus on the control and manufacturing the `pre === tapped` precondition for the NEXT action. There is one authored copy of each now, in runners/source/shared/dom-walk.mjs, composed host-side with new Function so Playwright can serialize it and WebDriver can interpolate its source; nothing is eval'd in the page, so an app's CSP is untouched. Six duplicate resolvers and three content-bug copies are gone and Electron's tap is byte-identical to the web runner's. Tauri's ACTIVATION is deliberately unchanged and stated in the source as a gap: WebDriver exposes a real pointer through the W3C Actions API, so it is unimplemented rather than unavailable, but proving it needs tauri-driver plus the platform webdriver, which this change had no way to drive, and a semantics change that cannot be run is a guess. No runtime guard can hold a defect that lives in the agreement BETWEEN two walks, so the bounded regression test is structural: one tappable grammar across every runner source, it is the shared one, a text field occupies a slot in it, no SHIPPED bundle still carries the rejected grammar (so a rebuild cannot smuggle it back), detectContentBugs has one authored copy, and the unkeyed tag-index fallback survives in every bundle that scans content. It is dependency-free so it runs in a bare worktree. The behavioural half runs the SHIPPED bundles in a real Chromium (runners/web/shared-dom-walk.test.mjs): it drives the actual Tauri snapshotJs walk and asserts every role-addressed selector it emits resolves through the shared resolver, that both transports agree, that the unkeyed artifact is reported, and that a real pointer click is blocked by an overlay while el.click() is not.",
